course: dictionary
lesson: 6451
title: Napkin, and words with more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A napkin is a square of cloth or paper that you use at meals to wipe your mouth and fingers and keep your clothes clean. The words around it give you clues. «And Grace hid her broccoli in her napkin» tells you the napkin is something you can hide food in, which fits a piece of cloth or paper.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "Grace hid her broccoli in her square of cloth" still makes sense, so your meaning is probably right. Grace's secret also tells you she did not want to eat the broccoli, and that is why she hid it.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. In some countries «napkin» once meant a baby's diaper. Look at the clues around the word. If the sentence talks about a baby or nappy changing, the old meaning fits. If it talks about a table or meals, the meaning is the everyday one.
fact: A napkin is a square piece of cloth or paper used at meals.
fact: You use a napkin to wipe your mouth and fingers and keep your clothes clean.
fact: Table manners: put the napkin on your lap, wipe your mouth gently, and never use your sleeve.
fact: In maths, folding a napkin can show halves, quarters and lines of symmetry.
fact: In some countries «napkin» (or «nappy») once meant a baby's diaper.
exercise: «And Grace hid her broccoli in her napkin.» What is a napkin? | *A square of cloth or paper for wiping your mouth at meals | A type of bread | A small bag for carrying books | A bowl for soup
exercise: Mum says, «Put your napkin on your lap.» Where does the napkin go? | On your head | *Across your lap | Under the table | In your pocket
exercise: In maths, folding a napkin in half shows what? | Spelling | Taste | *Halves | Loudness
test: Which phrase means a napkin made of paper? | *Paper napkin | Fold the napkin | Napkin on your lap | Cloth napkin
test: «Grace hid her broccoli in her napkin and said, All gone!» What was Grace doing? | *Hiding food she did not want to eat | Folding a napkin for maths | Cleaning her hands | Drying her hair
exam: In some countries, what did «napkin» once mean? | A kind of hat | Paper cloth for the table | A small bag for books | *A baby's diaper
